      "text": "We can write $150^\\circ$ as $360^\\circ - 210^\\circ$.\nSo, $\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos (360^\\circ - 210^\\circ)$.\nSince cosine is periodic with a period of $360^\\circ$, we have $\\cos (360^\\circ - 210^\\circ) = \\cos (-210^\\circ)$.\nNow, we can use the cosine addition formula, $\\cos (a + b) = \\cos a \\cos b - \\sin a \\sin b$.\nIn this case, let $a = -110^\\circ$ and $b = 10^\\circ$, so we have:\n$\\cos (-210^\\circ) = \\cos (-110^\\circ + 10^\\circ) = \\cos (-100^\\circ)$.\nNow, we can use the cosine addition formula again to get:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos (-10^\\circ + 20^\\circ) = \\cos (-10^\\circ) \\cos 20^\\circ - \\sin (-10^\\circ) \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nSince cosine is an even function, $\\cos (-10^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ$.\nAnd since sine is an odd function, $\\sin (-10^\\circ) = -\\sin 10^\\circ$.\nTherefore, we have:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nNow, we can use the angle addition formula for sine to get:\n$\\sin 20^\\circ = \\sin (10^\\circ + 10^\\circ) = \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ$.\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + (\\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ) \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nSimplifying, we have:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 20^\\circ + \\sin 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nNotice that the first two terms can be combined using the cosine addition formula.\nSo, we have:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ (\\cos 20^\\circ + \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ) + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nUsing the angle addition formula for sine again, we can rewrite the expression inside the parentheses as:\n$\\cos 20^\\circ + \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ = \\cos (20^\\circ - 10^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ$.\nTherefore, we have:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos 10^\\circ \\cos 10^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nSimplifying, we get:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) = \\cos^2 10^\\circ + \\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ$.\nFinally, we can use a calculator or trigonometric tables to find that:\n$\\cos^2 10^\\circ \\approx 0.9511$ and $\\cos 10^\\circ \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 20^\\circ \\approx 0.0212$.\nAdding these values, we get:\n$\\cos (-100^\\circ) \\approx 0.9723$.\nTherefore, $\\cos 150^\\circ = \\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$.The answer is: -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
