      "text": "To find $\\cos 150^\\circ$, we can use the fact that $150^\\circ$ is in the second quadrant where the cosine function is negative. We also note that $150^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 30^\\circ$. Using the co-function identity for cosine, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(180^\\circ - \\theta) = -\\cos(\\theta)\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = \\cos(180^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = -\\cos 30^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know that $\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus,\n\n\\[\n\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
